{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/heterogeneity--education",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "heterogeneity (education)"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "The dissimilarity of the members of a learning group with respect to relevant attributes (e.g. attainment, language, social background, gender)."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "As a sub-concept of difference-sensitive pedagogy the term is initially descriptive — it describes variation without itself evaluating it — but is frequently given a normative turn in school pedagogy (\"heterogeneity as opportunity\"). Heterogeneity is an observational category for the composition of groups and refers to the learning group / class (meso level of difference) . The principal hazard is reification: by marking certain attributes as \"heterogeneity-relevant,\" social distinctions may be produced and entrenched rather than merely depicted. The very idea of a \"normal\" learner against which variation is measured is historically constructed. Concerning the dimension of power the concept seems neutral-descriptive on the surface, yet the term is relative to the assumed homogeneity and thus tends to convey a perspective centred on norms and deficits."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "5.2.2"
  ],
